# 2026-09-08 (session 79): A DEFER-UNTIL, and the FIRST time this mechanism has
#   been used since 2026-08-19. Read the reason before extending the date.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED: the lone-CR line-accounting fix, which IS a real extraction
#   change. A CR that is not part of a CRLF terminates a line for Delphi, the
#   RAD Studio IDE, VS Code and .NET, but tree-sitter advances its row counter
#   on LF only -- so every line the indexer stored after such a byte was one
#   lower than the editor shows. Two files in the hashed surface:
#     src\preprocess\DRagLint.Preprocess.Types.pas  -- NormalizeLoneCR (new)
#     src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas       -- calls it before parsing
#   Stored POSITIONS therefore change for any indexed file holding a lone CR, so
#   a bump is genuinely owed. This is not an escape-hatch "cannot affect
#   extraction" entry; it is a postponement of a debt that is real.
#
#   WHY IT IS DEFERRED RATHER THAN BUMPED NOW. The bump is batched, by the
#   owner's standing instruction: INBOX-extractor-change-bank.md holds TWO rows
#   and only this one is built. The other -- M1b, a body-inline comment corpus
#   written at index time -- is unbuilt. Bumping today would bill the full ~5 h
#   re-parse of ~7,000 library files plus every project index and bank only half
#   the batch, which is exactly what PLAN-SESSION-79 forbids: "If they are not
#   both finished and guarded by then, do not bump and do not start the
#   reindex."
#
#   WHAT IS STALE MEANWHILE, stated plainly because a deferral is a live debt:
#   every existing index keeps the OLD (one-too-low) line numbers for the files
#   that contain a lone CR. Measured across all 34 indexes on this machine:
#   58 files of 11,370, of which only 7 are code and 6 of those are vendored --
#   ABC5, kbmMemTable, OmniThreadLibrary and a CAD library. Exactly one is code
#   this shop owns (DataCopy Tests\Test.UsageWiring.pas, one lone CR).
#
#   AND THE USER-VISIBLE HALF IS ALREADY FIXED WITHOUT THIS BUMP, which is why
#   deferring costs so little. There are THREE parse entry points and only one
#   is in this surface:
#     TDelphi13Parser.Parse       (src\parser)      -- the indexer   <- deferred
#     TLinter                     (src\lint)        -- own TTSParser <- SHIPPED
#     TAstParseCache.GetOrParse   (src\diagnostics) -- AST checks    <- SHIPPED
#   Every harm the defect note lists -- `allow --fix-line` writing a review
#   marker to the wrong line, gutter icons, the Problems panel,
#   review-marker-stale hashing the wrong line -- comes from the latter two,
#   which are OUTSIDE this surface and shipped today with no reindex. Both get
#   the transform in TAstParseCache.ApplyPreprocess.
#
#   THE TRANSITIONAL INCONSISTENCY, so it is not discovered as a surprise: until
#   the bump, `lint` reports the editor-correct line for a lone-CR file while the
#   INDEX still holds the old one. Two surfaces disagreeing by one line, on the
#   seven files above. That is the price of the deferral and it resolves on the
#   reindex.
#
#   THE DATE. PLAN-SESSION-79 has the owner kicking off the ~5 h reindex on
#   Tuesday evening 2026-09-09, with the bump immediately before it. 2026-09-10
#   gives that one day of slack so an overnight run cannot turn the battery red
#   mid-flight. The guard goes RED by itself on 2026-09-11, which is the point.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_lone_cr_line_accounting.ps1, which was
#   observed RED (13,17) against the pre-fix engine and green (14,18) after.

# 2026-09-08 (session 79): A REAL BUMP, 1.10.0-alpha -> 1.11.0-alpha, and it
#   DISCHARGES the DEFER-UNTIL|2026-09-10 recorded earlier the same day. Both
#   rows of INBOX-extractor-change-bank.md are now built and guarded, which was
#   the condition that deferral was waiting on -- so the bump is taken once, for
#   both, exactly as the bank exists to arrange.
#
#   WHAT CHANGED, and each genuinely invalidates stored parses:
#
#   1. LONE-CR LINE ACCOUNTING (src\preprocess\DRagLint.Preprocess.Types.pas,
#      new NormalizeLoneCR; called from src\parser\DRagLint.Parser.Delphi13.pas).
#      tree-sitter advances its row counter on LF only, so a CR that is not part
#      of a CRLF did not start a new line for it while Delphi, the IDE, VS Code
#      and .NET all treat it as a terminator. Every POSITION stored after such a
#      byte was one line low. 58 of 11,370 indexed files hold a lone CR.
#
#   2. A COMMENT CORPUS (same parser unit, HarvestStringLiterals). Comment nodes
#      are now harvested into string_literals as kind 'comment' (// and the block
#      forms) and 'doc' (triple-slash), so `query --text` finds prose. Those are
#      NEW ROWS that no existing index contains -- the clearest possible case of
#      "an extractor emits new symbols/refs", and unreachable without a re-parse.
#
#   NO SCHEMA_VERSION BUMP IS OWED. The comments are rows in the EXISTING
#   string_literals table, so the existing string_literals_ai trigger mirrors
#   them into both string_fts (unicode61) and string_fts_tri (trigram). No new
#   table, no new trigger, no new column.
#
#   SIZE, MEASURED RATHER THAN FEARED. On src\parser (5 files, this repo's own
#   heavily-commented source) comment text is 96.6% of all indexed text and
#   raises it 29x -- which sounds alarming and is the wrong denominator. The
#   DATABASE grows **1.5%** (2,179,072 -> 2,211,840 bytes, both VACUUMed), because
#   a DB is dominated by symbols, refs and their indexes, not by literal text.
#   Measured by deleting the comment rows from a copy and vacuuming, which fires
#   the same triggers and reproduces a comments-free index exactly.
#
#   THE RE-PARSE IS THE POINT, not a cost to argue away: until every index is
#   rebuilt, a lone-CR file answers with line numbers one low, and NO index can
#   answer a comment-prose query at all.
#
#   Pinned by tests\autotest\run_lone_cr_line_accounting.ps1 (RED 13,17 before /
#   green 14,18 after, with an LF twin as oracle) and
#   tests\autotest\run_comment_corpus.ps1 (RED hits=0 before / 16 checks green
#   after, with literal-vs-comment --kind controls both ways).
#
#   Superseded: 1.10.0-alpha|7562d480be39c02cdf165f9bf4edf888a158742de7a2b68468a0e1e2eff637d2

